[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Thinking: The user is migrating from Mapbox to MapLibre, removed access token, style URL mapbox:// won't load. They ask: "How can I test my map against a tile service that doesn't require an API key?" Need to explain using open tile services like OpenStreetMap, OpenFreeMap, MapTiler (free tier), OSM tiles, or using a simple style JSON referencing OSM raster or vector tiles. Provide steps: use a public style URL like https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty or use MapLibre demo style from maplibre.org, or use OSM vector tiles via open-source tile server like https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png for raster, or vector via https://tiles.stadiamaps.com/tiles/alidade_smooth/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf?key=... but that needs key. Provide example using MapLibre's own style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json' which uses vector tiles from open source. Show how to set style, maybe use Maputnik to edit. Also mention using PMTiles locally. Provide code snippet. Also mention using MapLibre's demo style and test.

Will answer with steps, example code, list of free services, how to verify style JSON, and note attribution.

Below is a quick‑start “cheat sheet” you can copy‑paste into a sandbox (CodeSandbox, StackBlitz, a local `index.html`, etc.) to see a **MapLibre map working with a tile source that needs **no API key**.  

It shows three common ways to get a style that will load out‑of‑the‑box:

| Option | What you get | URL you point `style` at | Does it need a key? | Attribution you must show |
|--------|--------------|--------------------------|---------------------|---------------------------|
| **MapLibre demo style** (vector) | Minimal “streets” style, uses the public `demotiles.maplibre.org` vector tiles | `https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json` | **No** | “© OpenStreetMap contributors” (the style already includes the required `<a>` tag) |
| **OpenFreeMap Liberty style** (vector) | Full‑featured community‑maintained style (similar to Mapbox “streets”) | `https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty` | **No** | “© OpenStreetMap contributors” (built‑in) |
| **Plain raster OSM tiles** (raster) | Very simple raster basemap (no vector style) | Use a custom style that points to `https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | **No** | “© OpenStreetMap contributors” (you must add it yourself) |

Below the table you’ll find a **complete HTML/JS example** that uses the first option (the MapLibre demo style). Swap the `style` URL for any of the other two options if you prefer.

---

## 1️⃣ Minimal HTML page (no build tools)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre test – no API key needed</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

  <!-- MapLibre CSS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />

  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    .attribution {
      position: absolute;
      bottom: 0;
      left: 0;
      margin: 0.5rem;
      font-size: 0.75rem;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.8);
      padding: 0.2rem 0.4rem;
      border-radius: 3px;
    }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
  <div id="map"></div>

  <!-- MapLibre JS – ES‑module version (v6+ only) -->
  <script type="module">
    import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: 'map',
      // 👉 1️⃣ Pick any of the URLs from the table above
      style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
      // style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty',
      // style: {
      //   version: 8,
      //   sources: {
      //     "raster-tiles": {
      //       type: "raster",
      //       tiles: ["https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
      //       tileSize: 256,
      //       attribution: "© OpenStreetMap contributors"
      //     }
      //   },
      //   layers: [{
      //     id: "osm-tiles",
      //     type: "raster",
      //     source: "raster-tiles"
      //   }]
      // },
      center: [-122.42, 37.78], // San Francisco
      zoom: 12,
      // optional: enable globe view (v5+)
      // projection: 'globe'
    });

    // Add the default navigation controls (zoom + compass)
    map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

    // Show the built‑in attribution (the style already injects it,
    // but we add a tiny fallback for the raster‑only example)
    map.on('load', () => {
      const att = document.createElement('div');
      att.className = 'attribution';
      att.innerHTML = map.getStyle().metadata?.attribution ||
                      '© <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors';
      document.body.appendChild(att);
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What this does

1. **Loads the ES‑module bundle** of MapLibre GL JS v6 from a CDN (`unpkg.com`).  
   - If you’re using a bundler (Webpack, Vite, etc.) you’d `npm i maplibre-gl` and `import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';` instead.
2. **Creates a `Map`** with a `style` that points to a publicly hosted JSON.  
   - The demo style (`demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json`) already references vector tiles hosted on the same domain, so there’s nothing else to configure.
3. **Adds a navigation control** (zoom buttons + compass) – the same API you used with Mapbox.
4. **Shows attribution** – required by the OSM data license. The demo style injects its own attribution element; the raster‑only example adds a tiny fallback.

---

## 2️⃣ If you want to **use your own style JSON** (e.g. a copy of a Mapbox Studio style)

1. **Export the style** from Mapbox Studio (download the JSON).  
2. **Replace every `source` that uses the `mapbox://` protocol** with a URL that points to a non‑Mapbox tile service.  
   - For vector tiles you can point to the public demo tiles: `https://demotiles.maplibre.org/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` (or any other vector tile endpoint you control).  
   - For raster tiles you can point to `https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png`.  
3. **Update the `sprite` and `glyphs` URLs** (Mapbox Studio defaults to `mapbox://sprites/...` and `mapbox://fonts/...`). Use a public sprite sheet or host your own. A quick‑and‑dirty way is to point to the demo sprite/glyphs:

   ```json
   "sprite": "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/sprite",
   "glyphs": "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf"
   ```

4. **Save the edited JSON** (e.g. `my-style.json`) and serve it from a static host (GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, or even a local dev server).  
5. **Pass that URL to `new maplibregl.Map({ style: '/my-style.json' })`.**

> **Tip:** Use the open‑source style editor **Maputnik** (`https://maputnik.github.io/`) to load the JSON, edit the source URLs, and instantly see if the map renders. It also validates the style against the MapLibre spec.

---

## 3️⃣ Quick list of **free, no‑key tile providers** you can point a style at

| Provider | Tile type | Example URL pattern | Notes |
|----------|-----------|---------------------|-------|
| **OpenStreetMap raster** | raster | `https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | Public tile server – **rate‑limited**; good for demos, not production. |
| **OpenFreeMap** | vector | `https://tiles.openfreemap.org/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` (used inside the Liberty style) | Community‑funded, no key. |
| **MapLibre demo tiles** | vector | `https://demotiles.maplibre.org/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` | Used by the demo style; stable for testing. |
| **Stadia Maps (free tier)** | raster/vector | `https://tiles.stadiamaps.com/tiles/alidade_smooth/{z}/{x}/{y}{r}.png` | Free tier requires an API key for higher usage; the base raster tiles work without a key but **check the terms**. |
| **Carto (basemap‑lite)** | raster | `https://{s}.basemaps.cartocdn.com/light_all/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | No key needed for low‑volume use. |
| **Esri World‑Imagery (public)** | raster | `https://services.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/World_Imagery/MapServer/tile/{z}/{y}/{x}` | Requires attribution to Esri. |
| **PMTiles (local or CDN)** | vector | `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@protomaps/pmtiles@latest/PMTiles.js` + `new PMTiles(...).getTile(z,x,y)` | Great for self‑hosted or static‑site tiles; see the `maplibre-pmtiles-patterns` skill for a full example. |

> **Production advice:** For any public tile service, read the provider’s usage policy. If you expect > 10 k tiles/day, self‑host (e.g., with **Martin**, **tileserver‑gl**, or **PMTiles**) or buy a commercial plan.

---

## 4️⃣ Checklist – “Why my map still isn’t loading?”

| ✅ | Check |
|----|-------|
| 1️⃣ | **Package** – `npm i maplibre-gl` (or CDN) – you’re no longer importing `mapbox-gl`. |
| 2️⃣ | **Namespace** – all `mapboxgl` → `maplibregl` (including CSS class prefixes). |
| 3️⃣ | **Access token** – removed. No `mapboxgl.accessToken` line. |
| 4️⃣ | **Style URL** – **not** `mapbox://…`. Use one of the URLs above or a custom JSON that points to non‑Mapbox sources. |
| 5️⃣ | **Sprite & glyph URLs** – also must be non‑Mapbox (the demo style already has them). |
| 6️⃣ | **CORS** – the tile server must allow cross‑origin requests from your domain (most public services do). |
| 7️⃣ | **Attribution** – you’ll see a console warning if the style is missing required OSM attribution. |
| 8️⃣ | **Console errors** – look for `Failed to load style` or `NetworkError when attempting to fetch resource`. The URL in the error tells you which resource is still pointing at `mapbox://`. |

If you still see a “style load error”, open the browser dev‑tools → **Network** tab, locate the failing request, and verify its URL. Most often it’s a stray `mapbox://fonts/...` or `mapbox://sprites/...` reference left in the style JSON.

---

## 5️⃣ TL;DR – One‑liner for a quick test

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // <-- free, no key
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
}).addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());
```

Paste that into a `<script type="module">` block (or a small React/Vite project) and you’ll have a fully functional map **without any API key**.

---

### 🎉 You’re ready

1. Swap the `style` URL to the free one you like.  
2. If you have a custom style, edit its source URLs as described.  
3. Run the page – you should see the map load instantly.  

From there you can continue migrating the rest of your app (plugins, geocoding, routing, etc.) while keeping the same MapLibre API you already know. Happy mapping! 🚀
